    s_FEMUR_RECORD(py_str("The thigh bone, the femur, is the strongest: it takes about 30 times your body weight lengthwise before it breaks, and it is also the longest bone in the body. It breaks across the shaft rather than along it, which is why a car bumper at knee height is such a common way to break one."));
    s_STAPES_RECORD(py_str("The stapes, one of the three tiny bones in the middle ear: about 3mm long and lighter than a grain of rice. It passes the vibration of the eardrum on to the inner ear, and the three ear bones together are the smallest in the body. Because they are so small, a hard blow to the side of the head can break them."));
    s_ENAMEL_RECORD(py_str("Tooth enamel, the outer layer of the teeth: harder than bone, and it is the only part of you that cannot repair itself, because it is not living tissue. That is why acid and grinding do permanent damage while a broken bone heals. Fluoride makes it a little more acid-resistant, which is most of what a fluoride toothpaste does."));
    s_TEETH_RECORD(py_str("An adult has 32 teeth including the four wisdom teeth, and a child has 20 milk teeth, which start arriving at about six months and are all replaced by about 12. The wisdom teeth come last, in the late teens or twenties, and roughly a third of people never get room for all four, which is why so many are removed."));
    s_MUSCLES_RECORD(py_str("About 600 skeletal muscles - the ones you can move on purpose - and something like 650 in all once the smooth muscle in the gut, blood vessels and bladder is counted. They are roughly 40% of your body weight, and the strongest for its size is the masseter, the muscle that closes the jaw."));
    s_FINGERPRINT_RECORD(py_str("Fingerprints are ridges that form in the womb, and they help in two ways: the ridges give grip on a wet or smooth surface, the way a treaded tyre does, and they put the touch receptors closer to whatever you are handling, which is why a fingertip can feel a hair on a page. The pattern is set before birth and is different even in identical twins."));
    s_FEMUR_LENGTH(py_str("The thigh bone, the femur, is the longest bone in the body - about a quarter of your height, so roughly 45cm in a 1.8m adult. The shortest is the stapes, deep in the ear, at about 3mm."));
    s_FEMUR_BIGGEST(py_str("The thigh bone, the femur, is the biggest bone in the body: about 45cm long in a 1.8m adult, a quarter of your height, and it takes roughly 30 times your body weight lengthwise before it breaks, which also makes it the strongest. It breaks across the shaft rather than along it, which is why a car bumper at knee height is such a common way to break one."));
    s_SARTORIUS_RECORD(py_str("The longest muscle is the sartorius, the thin one that runs from the outside of the hip across the front of the thigh to the inside of the knee - about 50cm, the longest in the body. It is the muscle that lets you sit cross-legged, and it is not a strong one: it is long and thin, and its job is to swing and turn the leg rather than to carry weight."));
    s_STRONGEST_MUSCLE(py_str("The strongest muscle for its size is the masseter, the one that closes your jaw: the back teeth can bite with about 70kg of force, and the muscle is the strongest there is per square centimetre. The most powerful overall is the gluteus maximus in your backside, the muscle that stands you up and drives you upstairs."));
    s_GLUTEUS_RECORD(py_str("The biggest muscle is the gluteus maximus, the large one in your backside: it is the most powerful muscle in the body and the one that straightens the hip when you stand up, climb stairs or run. The longest is a different one - the sartorius, the thin muscle that runs across the front of the thigh."));
