    s__BRUISE_PLAIN(py_str("A bruise is blood leaking under the skin from small vessels broken by a knock, which is why it turns purple, then green, then yellow as the blood is cleared away. It fades on its own in one to two weeks, and a cold pack for twenty minutes with a cloth round it, a few times in the first two days, keeps it from spreading and eases the throbbing. If a bruise turns up with no injury behind it, or you bruise far more easily than you used to, get it checked: that is worth a blood test."));
    s__BRUISE_SPREADING(py_str("A bruise does spread a little in the first day or two, as the blood that leaked tracks along under the skin, and the colour drifting outwards is that blood being cleared rather than a new injury. Cold for twenty minutes at a time, with a cloth round the pack, for the first day or two, keeps the swelling down. Get it looked at if it is still growing after two days, if it goes hard, tight and hot, or if it came up without a knock."));
    s__BRUISE_FASTER(py_str("Cold is what speeds a bruise up: a cold pack for twenty minutes at a time, a cloth round it, a few times a day for the first two days, then time - and raise the limb on a cushion when you sit. It still fades over one to two weeks through purple, green and yellow, and no cream honestly changes that; arnica has little evidence behind it. Take paracetamol rather than aspirin or ibuprofen, which thin the blood and can make the bruising worse."));
    s__BRUISE_STAYS(py_str("A bruise that is still there after two weeks has stopped being an ordinary bruise: blood under the skin is cleared away in one to two weeks, so one that will not fade, or that keeps coming back in the same place, is worth showing to a GP. The same goes for bruising that arrives with no knock behind it, or bruising far more easily than you used to - that is a blood test, and it is a quick one. While you wait, treat it as any bruise: cold for twenty minutes at a time, a cloth round the pack, for the first day or two."));
    s__BRUISE_HURTS(py_str("A bruise hurts because the blood that leaked under the skin is pressing on the tissue around it, and that tightness eases over the first day or two as the blood settles and starts to clear. Cold for twenty minutes at a time, with a cloth round the pack, takes the throb down, and paracetamol is the painkiller to reach for: aspirin and ibuprofen thin the blood and can make the bruise bigger. Get it seen if the pain is worse rather than better after two days, or the area goes hard, hot and tight."));
