    s_BUILDING_STANDS(py_str("A tall building stays up because its weight runs straight down a braced frame into deep foundations, so wind bends it a little instead of tipping it, and the tallest carry a heavy damper near the top to soak up the sway. Pisa leans without falling because its weight still sits over its base: it sank slowly into soft ground, and soil taken out from the high side is what steadied it."));
    s_CRANE_STANDS(py_str("A tower crane stays up because its mast is bolted to a concrete base and tied into the building as that rises, and a counterweight on the short arm balances the load on the long one. It is built to lift a set weight at a set reach and no more, so it trips out before it can tip."));
    s_BRIDGE_STANDS(py_str("A bridge gets its load to the ground in one of three ways: a beam rests the span on a support at each end, an arch pushes the weight outwards into solid abutments, and a suspension or cable-stayed bridge hangs the deck from cables over towers anchored into the ground. Whichever it is, the span is never left to carry itself."));
    s_CHIMNEY_STANDS(py_str("A chimney stays up because each stack of bricks is bonded into the wall it stands on and strapped to the roof, and every joint is staggered so the bricks cannot slide on each other - a straight stack of bricks with a flue down the middle is strong only while the mortar and the flaunching at the top are sound. That is why a chimney comes down in a storm, and why it comes down as one piece: the mortar in the joints has gone soft, or smoke and rain have washed it out from under the flaunching, so the whole stack rocks on its base."));
    s_TREE_STANDS(py_str("A tree stays up because its roots spread about as wide as its branches and the trunk tapers as it rises, so most of the weight is low down and the crown is light. Wind is what tests it: a tree that bends sheds the force, and the one that comes down is the one whose roots were cut on one side or left standing in waterlogged soil."));
    s_PLANT_STANDS(py_str("A houseplant flops over for one of three reasons: it has grown tall and thin reaching for light, the pot has become top-heavy for its roots, or the roots have rotted in soggy compost and let go. Move it somewhere brighter, put a cane or a hoop in and tie the stems loosely, and check the drainage holes before you water again - a pot standing in a full saucer is the usual cause."));
    s_GARDEN_PLANT_STANDS(py_str("A plant in the ground or a grow bag goes over for one of four reasons, and the fix is the one that matches: it grew tall and soft reaching for light, so stake it loosely with a cane and a soft tie in a figure of eight and give it more sun; it was sown too thickly and the stems are thin, so thin them next time; the roots have been eaten or rotted, so lift the sick one and look; or wind and rain bent it, so a windbreak or a row of sturdier plants on that side is what stops it next year. Seedlings that flop at soil level are damping off from cold wet compost rather than anything above it."));
