    s_STRUCTURE_ANSWERS(py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("longest bridge"), py_str("longest bridges"), py_str("longest bridge in the world")]), py_str("The longest bridge in the world is the Danyang-Kunshan Grand Bridge on the Beijing-Shanghai high-speed railway, about 165 km, most of it on piers over land and water. The longest over open sea is the Jiaozhou Bay Bridge at about 42 km, and the longest in Europe is the Crimean Bridge at about 19 km. A long bridge is nearly always a run of short spans on piers rather than one big span.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("suspension bridge"), py_str("suspension bridges")]), py_str("A suspension bridge hangs its deck from two main cables slung over the towers and anchored into the ground at each end, with vertical hangers dropping to the deck. The cables pull, the towers carry that pull down, and the anchors take it at the ends, which is why it can span 1 to 2 km - the Humber Bridge, the Golden Gate, the Akashi Kaikyo.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("cable stayed"), py_str("cable-stayed"), py_str("cable stayed bridge")]), py_str("A cable-stayed bridge runs cables straight from the tower to the deck, a fan or a harp of them, instead of one main cable with hangers. It needs no big anchorage at the ends, so it suits spans of a few hundred metres - the Queen Elizabeth II Bridge at Dartford and the Millau Viaduct are cable-stayed.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("box girder"), py_str("box girders"), py_str("box girder bridge")]), py_str("A box girder is a beam made as a hollow box of steel or concrete, wide at the top to carry the road and deep enough to be stiff: a hollow box is far stiffer than a solid bar of the same weight, because the material sits out at the edges. Most long concrete bridges and cable-stayed decks are a box girder, and the box is also where the cables and the air for the inside run.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("pier"), py_str("piers")]), py_str("A pier is one of the upright supports a bridge span rests on, and it is what the weight of the span comes down through to the ground. In a river they are built inside a cofferdam or a caisson, and the spans sit on bearings on top of them so the deck can expand and contract without cracking.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("beam bridge"), py_str("beam bridges"), py_str("girder bridge"), py_str("girder bridges")]), py_str("A beam bridge is the simplest kind there is: a slab or a girder laid across two supports. The deck bends under the load, so the top is squeezed and the bottom is stretched, and the deeper the girder the further it reaches. Cheap, quick, and right for a short span.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("arch bridge"), py_str("arch bridges"), py_str("how does an arch work"), py_str("how do arches work"), py_str("why do arches work")]), py_str("An arch bridge carries its load in compression: the weight comes down through the arch and pushes outwards at the bottom, so the ground or the abutments at each end have to resist that push. That is why stone and brick are so good in an arch - they take squeezing and not stretching - and why the Iron Bridge at Coalbrookdale, the first big cast-iron arch, was built in 1779 in the same shape.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("viaduct"), py_str("viaducts"), py_str("aqueduct"), py_str("aqueducts")]), py_str("A viaduct carries a road or a railway across a valley on a run of arches or piers rather than one span, which is what you need when the gap is long and the ground below is uneven - the Ribblehead Viaduct has 24 arches. An aqueduct is the same structure carrying water: the Romans built them on stone arches, and a canal aqueduct does the same job for boats.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("cantilever"), py_str("cantilevers"), py_str("cantilever bridge"), py_str("cantilever bridges")]), py_str("A cantilever is a beam held at one end with the other end free: a balcony, a diving board, a crane jib. Two cantilevers reaching out from opposite banks make a cantilever bridge, and it can be built outwards from each side without scaffolding in the water - the Forth Bridge is the famous one.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("expansion joint"), py_str("expansion joints")]), py_str("Bridges have expansion joints because steel and concrete change length as they warm and cool, and a long deck moves by several centimetres between winter and summer. The joint is the strip that lets it happen without cracking the deck or shoving the abutments - the toothed metal you hear clicking under the wheels - and it is also what lets the bridge move in an earthquake.")]), py_tuple(vec![py_tuple(vec![py_str("tunnel"), py_str("tunnels")]), py_str("A tunnel under water is usually not bored through the seabed: most are immersed tubes - a trench is dredged, boxed sections are floated out, sunk into it and joined, then covered over, which is how the Oresund link and the Fehmarn tunnel were built. Boring is used where the ground is good, as in the chalk under the Channel: a shielded machine cuts the face and the lining goes up behind it as it goes.")])]));
